# A staging environment, for trying migrations somewhere that does not
# matter.
#
# Copy this to .env.staging and fill in the two values from a SEPARATE
# Supabase project - never the live one. .env.staging is git-ignored;
# this example file is not, and must stay empty.
#
#   npm run dev:staging     the shop, against staging
#   npm run build:staging   a build, into dist-staging/
#
# Neither touches .env, dist/, or anything the real site uses.

# The staging project's URL and publishable key. Both are meant to be
# public and are compiled into the bundle, exactly as the live ones
# are. What they may do is decided by row-level security, which is
# part of what staging exists to test.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=


# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT HERE, and not anywhere with a VITE_ prefix.
#
# A service-role key bypasses every row-level security rule in the
# project. A VITE_ variable is compiled into JavaScript that anybody
# can read. Putting one in the other hands the database to the
# internet, and it is as true of a staging key as a live one - a
# staging database still has to be a fair test of the real rules.
#
# These belong to the Edge Functions and are set with
#   supabase secrets set NAME=value --project-ref <the staging ref>
#
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY   set by Supabase itself; never by hand
#   ALLOWED_ORIGINS             http://localhost:5173 while testing
#   TRUSTED_IP_HEADER           leave unset until the platform's own
#                               header is known - guessing it would
#                               make the rate limiter lie
#   EMAIL_PROVIDER              leave unset; unset means "none", and
#                               the code already says so honestly
#
#   R2_ACCOUNT_ID, R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID, R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, R2_BUCKET
#                               only if photo uploads are being tested
#                               in staging. Use a SEPARATE bucket. The
#                               live bucket holds customers' own
#                               photographs and has no business in a
#                               test.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Before the first statement runs in staging, check you are not in the
# live project:
#
#   select count(*) from public.orders;
#
# A fresh staging project answers "relation does not exist". If it
# answers with a number, stop - that is a database with orders in it.
